<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Deiniol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 584</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Deiniol</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Deiniol was a sixth-century Welsh churchman, traditionally regarded as the founder of the monastery of Bangor in Gwynedd and its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. According to tradition he was consecrated by Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubricius">Dyfrig</a> with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maelgwn-gwynedd">Maelgwn Gwynedd</a>, who granted the land. Bangor Cathedral, which stands on the site of his monastery, is dedicated to him, and several churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and Brittany bear his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-deiniol.jpg" alt="Saint Deiniol" width="266" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alan Fryer · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deiniol_Sant_Cadeirlan_Bangor_Cathedral_St_Deiniol_-_geograph.org.uk_-_593071.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
